  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices with determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- For any $A\\in S,$ we have $1\\cdot A=A.$\n- For any $A\\in S$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$\n- There is a matrix $A\\in S$ such that $\\frac{1}{2}(2A)\\ne A.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {a}_{ij}={a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For a matrix in $A\\in S,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(2A)=(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2)A=1\\cdot A=A\n$$\n\nsince the scalar multiplication of matrices is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
